1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:04,771
Two runs off. But fielding is where I can't
tell you the whole story.

2
00:00:04,821 --> 00:00:08,834
I'm building CricPredict: a cricket
prediction app for fans.

3
00:00:08,884 --> 00:00:11,000
Entertainment only, not betting.

4
00:00:11,350 --> 00:00:14,858
First ODI of India's 2026 tour of New
Zealand.

5
00:00:14,908 --> 00:00:21,200
Virat Kohli took a one-handed diving catch
at second slip to dismiss Nathan Smith.

6
00:00:21,250 --> 00:00:27,000
Rohit Sharma made a sharp run-out assist.
Moments like these swing a match.

7
00:00:27,350 --> 00:00:35,200
At 84/4, after 17 overs, CricPredict
predicted 199/7.

8
00:00:35,550 --> 00:00:37,042
New Zealand finished 185 all out:

9
00:00:37,092 --> 00:00:39,800
fourteen runs lower, but three wickets more
than predicted.

10
00:00:40,150 --> 00:00:45,400
Later, at 171/9 after 37.3 overs, it
predicted 183 all out.

11
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:53,300
Two runs off. Encouraging, but a much
shorter forecasting horizon.

12
00:00:53,650 --> 00:01:00,309
Did fielding explain the gap? I can't prove
it. But the model has room to improve:

13
00:01:00,359 --> 00:01:05,300
in my backtest on men's ODIs, its average
error is 41.5 runs.

14
00:01:05,650 --> 00:01:08,677
Fielding is one thing it can't see properly.

15
00:01:08,727 --> 00:01:15,600
Today it is just one hand-assigned rating
per team, a reputation, not who is actually
on the field.

16
00:01:15,950 --> 00:01:28,200
I think that is part of what's holding
accuracy back, on runs and on wickets.

17
00:01:28,550 --> 00:01:36,511
Public ball-by-ball data names a fielder
only when a wicket falls, including run-outs
from direct hits.

18
00:01:36,561 --> 00:01:44,600
It doesn't show dropped catches, runs saved
by brilliant effort, or how difficult a
catching chance was.

19
00:01:44,950 --> 00:01:46,258
So here is the plan.

20
00:01:46,308 --> 00:01:57,601
Start with what public data does give me,
the catches and run-outs that end in a
wicket, and build a first fielding model
from that in shadow mode, knowing its
limits.

21
00:01:57,651 --> 00:02:03,918
Then, if I can get ball-by-ball fielding
data, use it to rate the actual fielders in
each XI.

22
00:02:03,968 --> 00:02:08,332
Test the effect on runs and wickets
separately, on fresh matches.

23
00:02:08,382 --> 00:02:12,000
If it doesn't beat the current model, it
doesn't ship.

24
00:02:12,350 --> 00:02:16,622
If you hold fielding data, or work at a data
provider, message me.

25
00:02:16,672 --> 00:02:19,700
Try CricPredict at
cricket-score-predictor.com.
